\begin_layout Section
Model 1.1 with AVR
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For generator 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

, the state order and its corresponding derivative order are 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
x_i=\bigl[\delta_i,\ \omega_i,\ E'_{qi},\ E'_{di},\ E_{fdi}\bigr]^T,
\qquad
\dot{x}_i=\bigl[\dot\delta_i,\ \dot\omega_i,\ \dot E'_{qi},\ \dot E'_{di},\ \dot E_{fdi}\bigr]^T
=f_i(x_i,I_{di},I_{qi},|V_i|,u_i),
\end{equation}
\end_inset

where the overdot denotes the time derivative. The complete input-driven DAE is 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\dot x=f(x,y,u),\qquad 0=g(x,y,u),
\label{eq:input-dae}
\end{equation}
\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\begin{aligned}
y&=\begin{bmatrix}\Re(V)^T&\Im(V)^T\end{bmatrix}^{T},\qquad
u=\begin{bmatrix}P_m^T&u_{exc}^{T}&\rho\end{bmatrix}^{T},\\
u_{exc}&=
\begin{cases}V_{ref},&\text{AVR},\\E_{fd0},&\text{manual excitation}.
\end{cases}
\end{aligned}
\label{eq:xyu-order}
\end{equation}
\end_inset

Thus 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 contains differential machine/AVR states, 
\begin_inset Formula $y$
\end_inset

 contains algebraic bus voltages, and 
\begin_inset Formula $u$
\end_inset

 contains prescribed inputs: mechanical power 
\begin_inset Formula $P_m$
\end_inset

, excitation command 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{exc}$
\end_inset

, and topology selector 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho$
\end_inset

. Equilibrium and SSSA hold 
\begin_inset Formula $u=u_0$
\end_inset

 fixed; TS changes only 
\begin_inset Formula $\rho(t)$
\end_inset

 at the declared event times. Generator current is positive from the machine into the network. The dynamic equations implemented in the shared DAE are 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{align}
\dot\delta_i &= \omega_B(\omega_i-1),\\
2H_i\dot\omega_i &= P_{mi}-P_{ei}-D_i(\omega_i-1),\\
T'_{d0i}\dot E'_{qi} &= E_{fdi}-E'_{qi}-(X_{di}-X'_{di})I_{di},\\
T'_{q0i}\dot E'_{di} &= -E'_{di}+(X_{qi}-X'_{qi})I_{qi},\\
T_{Ai}\dot E_{fdi} &= K_{Ai}(V_{ref,i}-|V_i|)-E_{fdi}.
\end{align}
\end_inset

The transient-saliency stator interface is 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{align}
V_{di}&=E'_{di}-R_aI_{di}+X'_qI_{qi},\\
V_{qi}&=E'_{qi}-R_aI_{qi}-X'_dI_{di}.
\end{align}
\end_inset

Air-gap electrical power is calculated directly as 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
P_{ei}=V_{di}I_{di}+V_{qi}I_{qi}+R_a(I_{di}^2+I_{qi}^2).
\end{equation}
\end_inset

The same nonlinear functions are used for equilibrium, SSSA and TS.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
AVR versus manual excitation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Two excitation configurations are studied from the same DAE: 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
With AVR (5 states/machine, 20 total).
\series default
 The field voltage 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{fd}$
\end_inset

 is a dynamic state governed by the single-time-constant AVR of Table
\begin_inset space ~

\end_inset

9.4 (
\begin_inset Formula $K_A=200$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $T_A=0.02$
\end_inset


\begin_inset space ~

\end_inset

s): 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
  T_A\dot E_{fd}=K_A(V_{ref}-|V|)-E_{fd}.
  \label{eq:avr-time}
  \end{equation}
\end_inset

At equilibrium 
\begin_inset Formula $\dot E_{fd}=0$
\end_inset

; therefore the voltage error is not zero but is exactly the field voltage divided by the gain: 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
  E_{fd0}=K_A(V_{ref}-|V_0|),\qquad
  V_{ref}=|V_0|+\frac{E_{fd0}}{K_A}.
  \label{eq:avr-equilibrium}
  \end{equation}
\end_inset

Linearising 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:avr-time"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

 with 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta V_{ref}=0$
\end_inset

 gives 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
  \Delta E_{fd}(s)=-\frac{K_A}{1+sT_A}\Delta|V|(s).
  \label{eq:avr-transfer}
  \end{equation}
\end_inset

Hence 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta|V|<0$
\end_inset

 produces 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta E_{fd}>0$
\end_inset

. The DC correction gain is 200 and the AVR pole is 
\begin_inset Formula $-1/T_A=-50~\mathrm{s}^{-1}$
\end_inset

, mathematically explaining its fast voltage-support action. This is the 
\emph on
configuration published in the book
\emph default
: Table
\begin_inset space ~

\end_inset

9.5 lists exactly 20 eigenvalues, matching the 
\begin_inset Formula $4\times5=20$
\end_inset

 state count. The inter-area swing mode is nevertheless very lightly damped. For 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda=\sigma+j\omega_d$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
  \zeta=-\frac{\sigma}{\sqrt{\sigma^2+\omega_d^2}}.
  \label{eq:damping-ratio}
  \end{equation}
\end_inset

The computed AVR pole 
\begin_inset Formula $-0.0042+j4.4565$
\end_inset

 therefore gives 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
  \zeta_{AVR}=\frac{0.0042}{\sqrt{0.0042^2+4.4565^2}}
  =9.5\times10^{-4}=0.095\%.
  \end{equation}
\end_inset

The lightly damped conclusion thus follows from the computed pole. 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Manual excitation/no AVR (4 states/machine, 16 total).
\series default
 Removing the AVR does not remove the generator field circuit. Instead, the operator-supplied field voltage is frozen at the equilibrium value: 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
  E_{fd}(t)=E_{fd0},\qquad \Delta E_{fd}(s)=0.
  \label{eq:manual-field}
  \end{equation}
\end_inset

The manual state and input orders are therefore 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
  x_i^{manual}=
  \begin{bmatrix}\delta_i&\omega_i&E'_{qi}&E'_{di}\end{bmatrix}^{T},
  \qquad
  u_i^{manual}=
  \begin{bmatrix}P_{mi}&E_{fd0,i}&\rho\end{bmatrix}^{T}.
  \label{eq:manual-state-input}
  \end{equation}
\end_inset

The four retained differential equations are 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{align}
  \dot\delta_i&=\omega_B(\omega_i-1),\\
  \dot\omega_i&=
  \frac{P_{mi}-P_{ei}-D_i(\omega_i-1)}{2H_i},\\
  \dot E'_{qi}&=
  \frac{E_{fd0,i}-E'_{qi}-(X_{di}-X'_{di})I_{di}}{T'_{d0i}},\\
  \dot E'_{di}&=
  \frac{-E'_{di}+(X_{qi}-X'_{qi})I_{qi}}{T'_{q0i}}.
  \label{eq:manual-dynamics}
  \end{align}
\end_inset

Thus manual excitation still has rotor, speed, 
\begin_inset Formula $d$
\end_inset

-axis flux, 
\begin_inset Formula $q$
\end_inset

-axis flux, stator-current, and network-voltage dynamics. What disappears is only the closed-loop field-voltage state and its terminal-voltage feedback.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Standard
Linearising the third equation in 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:manual-dynamics"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

 gives 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
  \Delta\dot E'_q=
  -\frac{1}{T'_{d0}}\Delta E'_q
  -\frac{X_d-X'_d}{T'_{d0}}\Delta I_d
  +\frac{1}{T'_{d0}}\underbrace{\Delta E_{fd}}_{0}.
  \label{eq:manual-linear-field}
  \end{equation}
\end_inset

Compare this with the AVR perturbation obtained from 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:avr-time"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

: 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
  \Delta\dot E_{fd}
  =-\frac{1}{T_A}\Delta E_{fd}
   -\frac{K_A}{T_A}\Delta|V|
   +\frac{K_A}{T_A}\Delta V_{ref}.
  \label{eq:avr-linear-field}
  \end{equation}
\end_inset

With 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta V_{ref}=0$
\end_inset

, the AVR introduces the coupling 
\begin_inset Formula $-(K_A/T_A)\Delta|V|$
\end_inset

 and one additional state. Manual excitation has neither term. In state-matrix form this means 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
  \begin{aligned}
  \Delta\dot x_{manual}&=A_{manual}\Delta x_{manual},
  &A_{manual}&\in\mathbb{R}^{16\times16},\\
  \Delta\dot x_{AVR}&=A_{AVR}\Delta x_{AVR},
  &A_{AVR}&\in\mathbb{R}^{20\times20}.
  \end{aligned}
  \end{equation}
\end_inset

No-AVR also does not mean constant terminal voltage: 
\begin_inset Formula $V$
\end_inset

 remains an algebraic solution of 
\begin_inset Formula $g(x,y,u)=0$
\end_inset

 and changes whenever machine current or network topology changes. Only 
\begin_inset Formula $E_{fd}$
\end_inset

 is constant.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This manual case is a separate 16-state study; it does 
\emph on
not
\emph default
 reproduce the 20-state Table
\begin_inset space ~

\end_inset

9.5. Its computed inter-area pole 
\begin_inset Formula $-0.1173+j4.0712$
\end_inset

 gives 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
  \zeta_{manual}=\frac{0.1173}{\sqrt{0.1173^2+4.0712^2}}
  =0.0288=2.88\%.
  \end{equation}
\end_inset

The comparison is consequently equation-based: the AVR improves voltage regulation through 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:avr-transfer"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

, while adding the feedback block 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:avr-linear-field"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

 changes the full system matrix and moves this inter-area pole from 
\begin_inset Formula $\zeta=2.88\%$
\end_inset

 to 
\begin_inset Formula $\zeta=0.095\%$
\end_inset

. This observation applies to the stated operating point and printed AVR parameters; it is not a universal claim that every AVR reduces damping. 
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Standard
The book uses the AVR throughout Section
\begin_inset space ~

\end_inset

9.6.1: Table
\begin_inset space ~

\end_inset

9.4 supplies the AVR parameters and Table
\begin_inset space ~

\end_inset

9.5 reports the 20-state eigenvalue spectrum. The manual case is a project-defined comparison, not a book configuration.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section
Small-signal stability
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The complete DAE is linearised numerically around the in-house power-flow operating point. Algebraic network-voltage perturbations are eliminated with a Schur complement. The published eigenvalues are transcribed in the table below (Source data); they are not copied into the computed column and are not used to tune parameters.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Linearisation and Schur reduction
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The nonlinear DAE in 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:input-dae"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

 is linearised about 
\begin_inset Formula $(x_0,y_0,u_0)$
\end_inset

 after verifying 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\|f(x_0,y_0,u_0)\|_\infty\le\varepsilon_{eq},\qquad
\|g(x_0,y_0,u_0)\|_\infty\le\varepsilon_{eq}.
\end{equation}
\end_inset

SSSA fixes the external input, 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta u=0$
\end_inset

. Substituting 
\begin_inset Formula $x=x_0+\Delta x$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $y=y_0+\Delta y$
\end_inset

 and retaining first-order terms gives 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\begin{bmatrix}\Delta\dot x\\0\end{bmatrix}
=
\begin{bmatrix}J_{xx}&J_{xy}\\J_{yx}&J_{yy}\end{bmatrix}
\begin{bmatrix}\Delta x\\\Delta y\end{bmatrix}.
\label{eq:sssa-linear-dae}
\end{equation}
\end_inset

The Jacobian blocks are 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
J_{xx}=\tfrac{\partial f}{\partial x},\quad
J_{xy}=\tfrac{\partial f}{\partial y},\quad
J_{yx}=\tfrac{\partial g}{\partial x},\quad
J_{yy}=\tfrac{\partial g}{\partial y}.
\end{equation}
\end_inset

They are evaluated by central differences. For example, 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{align}
J_{xx}(:,k)&\approx
\frac{f(x_0+h e_k,y_0,u_0)-f(x_0-h e_k,y_0,u_0)}{2h},\\
J_{yx}(:,k)&\approx
\frac{g(x_0+h e_k,y_0,u_0)-g(x_0-h e_k,y_0,u_0)}{2h},
\end{align}
\end_inset

with 
\begin_inset Formula $h=10^{-6}$
\end_inset

; 
\begin_inset Formula $J_{xy}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $J_{yy}$
\end_inset

 use 
\begin_inset Formula $y_0\pm h e_k$
\end_inset

. These are derivatives of the DAE equations, not derivatives of the reference table.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The algebraic row is solved as 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
J_{yy}\Delta y=-J_{yx}\Delta x.
\end{equation}
\end_inset

The code computes 
\begin_inset Formula $K=J_{yy}\backslash J_{yx}$
\end_inset

, avoiding an explicit inverse, and substitutes 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta y=-K\Delta x$
\end_inset

 into the differential row: 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
A=J_{xx}-J_{xy}K
=J_{xx}-J_{xy}J_{yy}^{-1}J_{yx},\qquad
\Delta\dot x=A\Delta x.
\label{eq:sssa-schur}
\end{equation}
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Eigenvalue solution and modal quantities
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The solver then evaluates 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
A v_i=\lambda_i v_i,\qquad
\det(\lambda_iI-A)=0,qquad \lambda_i=\sigma_i+j\omega_{d,i}.
\end{equation}
\end_inset

For each conjugate pair, 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
f_i=\frac{|\omega_{d,i}|}{2\pi},\qquad
\zeta_i=-\frac{\sigma_i}{\sqrt{\sigma_i^2+\omega_{d,i}^2}},\qquad
\tau_i=-\frac{1}{\sigma_i}\quad(\sigma_i<0).
\label{eq:sssa-modal-metrics}
\end{equation}
\end_inset

A physical mode is asymptotically stable when 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_i<0$
\end_inset

. Table
\begin_inset space ~

\end_inset

9 uses the full 20-state AVR matrix, matching the original-matrix column of Padiyar Table
\begin_inset space ~

\end_inset

9.5. The optional COI reduction is explicitly disabled in this execution, so the rotational reference-angle mode remains near the origin and is not misclassified as an unstable physical mode. Table
\begin_inset space ~

\end_inset

10 selects the positive-imaginary roots with 
\begin_inset Formula $1<\omega_d<10$
\end_inset

 rad/s; the omitted conjugates have identical 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\zeta$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Reference matching occurs only after 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:sssa-schur"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

 has been solved. One-to-one assignment minimizes 
\begin_inset Formula $|\lambda_{ours}-\lambda_{book}|$
\end_inset

 without changing 
\begin_inset Formula $A$
\end_inset

 or a model parameter. The reported errors are 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\varepsilon_\lambda=
100\frac{|\lambda_{ours}-\lambda_{book}|}{|\lambda_{book}|},\quad
\varepsilon_f=100\frac{|f_{ours}-f_{book}|}{f_{book}},\quad
\varepsilon_\zeta=
100\frac{|\zeta_{ours}-\zeta_{book}|}{|\zeta_{book}|}.
\end{equation}
\end_inset

The eigenvalue percentage is omitted for the near-zero reference pair because its denominator makes a relative error numerically meaningless.
\end_layout

