\begin_layout Section
Transient simulation
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The source pages specify no transient-fault case. For a controlled comparison, this report defines a project scenario: a temporary balanced fault at bus
\begin_inset space ~

\end_inset

3, 
\begin_inset Formula $Z_f=j0.5$
\end_inset

 pu, applied at 1.0
\begin_inset space ~

\end_inset

s and cleared at 1.1
\begin_inset space ~

\end_inset

s, with 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta t=0.005$
\end_inset


\begin_inset space ~

\end_inset

s. The same network, operating point and disturbance are used for manual and AVR configurations. The method uses a fixed nominal time step, exact event samples, and an iterative implicit-trapezoidal corrector.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Implicit trapezoidal integration
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The topology input is piecewise constant: 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
Y(t)=
\begin{cases}
Y_{pre},&t<t_f,\\
Y_{fault},&t_f\le t<t_c,\\
Y_{post},&t\ge t_c,
\end{cases}
\qquad
Y_{fault}=Y_{pre}+\frac{1}{Z_f}e_fe_f^T.
\label{eq:ts-topology}
\end{equation}
\end_inset

Here 
\begin_inset Formula $e_f$
\end_inset

 selects the faulted bus. Times 
\begin_inset Formula $t_f$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $t_c$
\end_inset

 are inserted exactly into the nominal 
\begin_inset Formula $\Delta t$
\end_inset

 grid, so no step hides a topology transition.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
At each accepted state 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

, the network voltage is obtained from 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
g(x,y,Y)=0.
\label{eq:ts-algebraic}
\end{equation}
\end_inset

For algebraic Newton iteration 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

, set 
\begin_inset Formula $r_g^{(k)}=g(x,y^{(k)},Y)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $G_y^{(k)}=\partial g/\partial y$
\end_inset

. Then 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
G_y^{(k)}\Delta y^{(k)}=-r_g^{(k)},\qquad
y^{(k+1)}=y^{(k)}+\alpha_k\Delta y^{(k)}.
\label{eq:ts-network-newton}
\end{equation}
\end_inset

The line search tries 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha_k=1,1/2,1/4,\ldots,2^{-16}$
\end_inset

 and accepts the first value that decreases 
\begin_inset Formula $\|r_g\|_\infty$
\end_inset

. The algebraic solve requires 
\begin_inset Formula $\|r_g\|_\infty\le10^{-11}$
\end_inset

 and refreshes 
\begin_inset Formula $G_y$
\end_inset

 by central differences if a step is rejected.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For 
\begin_inset Formula $h=t_{n+1}-t_n$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $f_n=f(x_n,y_n,u_n)$
\end_inset

, the predictor is 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\hat x^{(0)}_{n+1}=x_n+h f_n,\qquad
\hat y^{(0)}_{n+1}=y_n.
\label{eq:ts-predictor}
\end{equation}
\end_inset

Corrector iteration 
\begin_inset Formula $c$
\end_inset

 performs the following sequence: 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{align}
g(\hat x^{(c)}_{n+1},\hat y^{(c)}_{n+1},Y_n)&=0,\\
x^{(c+1)}_{n+1}
 &=x_n+\frac{h}{2}\left[f_n+
 f(\hat x^{(c)}_{n+1},\hat y^{(c)}_{n+1},u_n)\right],\\
g(x^{(c+1)}_{n+1},y^{(c+1)}_{n+1},Y_n)&=0,\\
R^{(c+1)}_{trap}
 &=x^{(c+1)}_{n+1}-x_n-\frac{h}{2}\left[f_n+
 f(x^{(c+1)}_{n+1},y^{(c+1)}_{n+1},u_n)\right].
\label{eq:ts-corrector}
\end{align}
\end_inset

The next iterate uses 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat x^{(c+1)}_{n+1}=x^{(c+1)}_{n+1}$
\end_inset

. Acceptance requires both 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\|x^{(c+1)}_{n+1}-\hat x^{(c)}_{n+1}\|_\infty\le\tau_n,
\qquad
\|R^{(c+1)}_{trap}\|_\infty\le\tau_n,
\end{equation}
\end_inset

where the implemented mixed tolerance is 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
\tau_n=10^{-10}+10^{-8}\max\left(1,\|x^{(c+1)}_{n+1}\|_\infty\right).
\end{equation}
\end_inset

At most 12 corrector iterations are allowed. A failed corrector raises an error instead of accepting the step. After acceptance, 
\begin_inset Formula $Y(t_{n+1})$
\end_inset

 is selected again and 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:ts-algebraic"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

 is solved, ensuring that a stored event sample is consistent with the new topology.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection
Fixed corrector iteration versus adaptive time stepping
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The corrector iteration and the time-step controller solve different problems. For fixed 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

, repeated correction drives the nonlinear equation residual 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
F_h(x_{n+1})=
x_{n+1}-x_n-\frac{h}{2}\left(f_n+f_{n+1}\right)
\end{equation}
\end_inset

toward zero. Thus 
\begin_inset Formula $\|F_h\|_\infty\ll1$
\end_inset

 proves that the trapezoidal equation for the chosen 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

 was solved accurately. It does not estimate the discretization error 
\begin_inset Formula $\|x(t_{n+1})-x_{n+1}\|$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
An adaptive second-order trapezoidal method additionally needs an independent local-error estimate. Step doubling would compute one full step 
\begin_inset Formula $x_h$
\end_inset

 and two half steps 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{h/2,h/2}$
\end_inset

, then form 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
e_n=\frac{x_{h/2,h/2}-x_h}{2^2-1},\qquad
E_n=\left\|
\frac{e_n}{a_{tol}+r_{tol}\max(|x_n|,|x_{h/2,h/2}|)}
\right\|_\infty.
\label{eq:adaptive-estimator}
\end{equation}
\end_inset

The step is accepted only when 
\begin_inset Formula $E_n\le1$
\end_inset

; otherwise it is rejected and recomputed. A typical controller is 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
h_{new}=h\,
\operatorname{clip}\left(f_{min},f_{max},
safety\,E_n^{-1/3}\right),
\label{eq:adaptive-controller}
\end{equation}
\end_inset

with event times imposing an upper bound on 
\begin_inset Formula $h_{new}$
\end_inset

 so the solver still lands exactly on 
\begin_inset Formula $t_f$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $t_c$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The present validation solver intentionally remains fixed-step because the published comparison protocol requires a deterministic common output grid and the implementation currently has neither the independent estimator 
\begin_inset CommandInset ref
LatexCommand eqref
reference "eq:adaptive-estimator"
plural "false"
caps "false"
noprefix "false"
nolink "false"
tuple "list"

\end_inset

 nor step rejection. Calling corrector iteration adaptive would therefore be false. Fixed 
\begin_inset Formula $h=0.005$
\end_inset


\begin_inset space ~

\end_inset

s is accepted here only after equilibrium, residual, convergence, and finite-trajectory checks; it is not presented as a general proof of time-discretization accuracy. Adaptive stepping is a valid future production improvement, but it must add the estimator, accept/reject controller, exact event landing, resampling for cross-tool comparison, and convergence tests under successively tighter tolerances before replacing this reproducible validation path.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The recorded outputs are reconstructed from the accepted 
\begin_inset Formula $(x_n,y_n)$
\end_inset

: 
\begin_inset Formula \begin{equation}
|V_b|=\sqrt{y_{2b-1}^2+y_{2b}^2},\qquad
P_{ei}=V_{di}I_{di}+V_{qi}I_{qi}+R_a(I_{di}^2+I_{qi}^2),
\end{equation}
\end_inset

with 
\begin_inset Formula $P_{ei}[\mathrm{MW}]=S_{base}[\mathrm{MVA}]P_{ei}[\mathrm{pu}]$
\end_inset

. The saved diagnostics include the initial DAE residual, corrector update norm, trapezoidal residual, iteration count, convergence flag, and non-converged-step count; consequently, a plausible plot cannot conceal a failed numerical step.
\end_layout

